title: '[DEFERRED] refine relay discovery privacy, scope, and introduction semantics'
---
The original premise is obsolete: relay discovery now exists.

`c2c relay list` lists live relay registrations and usable `<alias>@<host_id>` addresses. `c2c list --relay` merges those unknown relay peers with local peers and distinguishes local, relay, and scope-both identities. On production relays, the live-peer `/list` route requires a registered Ed25519 identity. Default `c2c list` remains local-only, and `c2c find` does not search the relay.

Relay rooms provide a second, narrower discovery surface: anonymous `relay rooms list` exposes public and gated rooms. Room rosters use `<alias>#<room>@relay` presentation addresses and deliberately hide host, lease, session, and identity-key metadata; these addresses support room interaction but are not direct-DM identities.

Discovery is not trust. Presence in either directory never makes an unknown alias trusted.

The remaining deferred product question is whether the current authenticated whole-live-peer directory is acceptable or should evolve toward explicit opt-in listing, introductions, scoped search, or stronger anti-spam/privacy controls. B009 and B010 are done; coordinate any future design with the relay ingress/rate controls and B207 advisory trust model that now exist.
